+    <section class="journal" aria-labelledby="journal-title">
+      <p class="eyebrow">From origin to your cup</p>
+      <h2 id="journal-title">Roaster's Journal</h2>
+      <article id="journal-copy" class="journal-copy" aria-label="Our bean sourcing process">
+        <p>A good cup begins long before we switch on the roaster. Our sourcing process starts with a simple question: who grew this coffee, and what makes their harvest special? We look for beans with a clear story, working with producers, cooperatives, and importers who can trace a lot back to its origin. Learning about the farm, the growing conditions, and the people behind each harvest helps us choose coffees with both character and a connection to the place they came from.</p>
+        <p>Before we commit to a coffee, we taste small samples side by side. We brew them without milk or sugar so we can pay attention to sweetness, acidity, body, and finish. One lot might remind us of ripe berries, while another brings the comforting depth of cocoa and toasted nuts. We also ask how the cherries were picked, how the beans were processed, and how they were dried. These details help us understand the flavor in the cup and decide which coffees deserve a place on our seasonal menu.</p>
+        <p>Flavor is only part of the conversation. We ask our sourcing partners about producer payments, working conditions, and farming practices, including how water and soil are cared for. We value clear answers over sweeping promises, and we look for partners willing to share information and keep improving. Whenever possible, we return to coffees from producers we already know. Building those relationships over successive harvests gives us a better understanding of their work and helps us plan purchases with more care.</p>
+        <p>Once a shipment arrives, we taste it again to make sure it matches the sample we selected. The green beans are stored in a cool, dry space, away from light and moisture, until they are ready for roasting. We then develop a roast profile in small batches, adjusting time and temperature to bring out the qualities we loved at the cupping table. Our aim is to let each coffee's origin come through, whether it is destined for a bright filter brew or a rich, balanced espresso.</p>
+        <p>Sourcing is an ongoing practice rather than a once-a-year decision. Harvests change with the seasons, and we keep tasting, asking questions, and refining our choices. That is why our offerings evolve: we would rather introduce a fresh seasonal lot than pretend every coffee tastes the same all year. By the time a bag reaches our counter, it has passed through many careful hands. Our job is to honor that work with a thoughtful roast and a cup worth slowing down for.</p>
+      </article>
